# Line endings are normalised to LF in the repository.
#
# This is not cosmetic. Development happens on Windows and production is Linux
# containers (docs/INFRASTRUCTURE.md), and `core.autocrlf=true` would commit
# CRLF into files that are executed there. `backend/scripts/backup.sh` is the
# clearest casualty: its shebang becomes `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r`, and the kernel
# then reports "no such file or directory" naming an interpreter that plainly
# exists — an hour lost to a character nobody can see.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only launchers. cmd.exe is the one consumer that genuinely wants CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary. Never diffed, never normalised, never corrupted by an eol rule.
*.stl   binary
*.3mf   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.dump  binary
